From 869605b2edf753ddfc45432d0a62ccab61fddd5e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dispatch Sandbox <jax1313@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 20:04:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/12] Put a provider-neutral transport behind the mail path,
 with Microsoft-compatible auth

Two findings, neither about code quality.

dispatch/connectors/ is ~3,000 lines across eight connectors and contains no HTTP
client at all -- no requests, no httpx, no urllib, no msal. "Eight connectors
IMPLEMENTED" was true and meant something narrower than it reads: the boundary is
real and the transport was never written.

And the two auth methods already chosen are the two Microsoft is closing.
outlook_connector.py declares oauth_client_credentials -- app-only Graph, which
needs an Entra ID tenant, CANNOT authenticate a personal Microsoft account at
all, and grants tenant-wide mailbox access to read one operator's calendar.
cin_lite/email_delivery.py sends with smtplib.login on port 587 -- SMTP AUTH
basic authentication, which Microsoft 365 disables by default on every tenant and
is retiring. The one path that actually reaches brokers is built on the mechanism
being switched off.

So: a port (OutboundMessage -> MessageTransport -> TransportResult) and four
adapters -- Graph sendMail, SMTP XOAUTH2, SMTP basic, and the .eml outbox --
each answering status() in the eight words before anything is sent, so a surface
renders what this machine will do rather than a claim about it. Nothing
configured selects the outbox and says SIMULATED, which is what Dispatch does
today and what it will keep saying until somebody configures a provider.

dispatch/msauth.py implements delegated device code: Dispatch prints a code, the
operator signs in as themselves, the token carries their own permissions. It
works on a personal outlook.com account and needs no client secret on the laptop
-- there is deliberately no DISPATCH_MS_CLIENT_SECRET setting anywhere. Scopes
are least-privilege and Mail.Read is not among them, because the program does not
read the operator's mailbox and the scope should say so.

cin_lite still imports nothing from dispatch. The newer transports register INTO
its one send function (dispatch/outbound.py), and only when one of them is
selected -- an install on plain SMTP keeps its exact code path, receipts and
fallback file.

NOT PROVEN, and stated as such in docs/connectors/MICROSOFT_365_ACTIVATION.md:
no Microsoft call has been made from this repository. The tests drive the
protocol through injected posters and openers. They prove request shapes,
polling rules, expiry arithmetic and refusal behaviour. They do not prove
connectivity and none of them pretends to.
